WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.118
Trust is a willingness to be vulnerable to someone because

00:00:03.118 --> 00:00:06.235
you expect them to act well. Staff show it when

00:00:06.235 --> 00:00:09.353
they raise a problem with you, accept an uncertain assignment,

00:00:09.353 --> 00:00:12.471
or plan around something you promised. Large reviews of the

00:00:12.471 --> 00:00:15.588
research link trust in leaders to better performance and commitment.

00:00:15.588 --> 00:00:18.706
And direct supervisors, the people staff work with every day,

00:00:18.706 --> 00:00:21.200
are an especially important source of that trust.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.209
People decide whether to trust you on three things. Can

00:00:25.209 --> 00:00:28.869
you do the job? Do you care about them? Do

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:32.528
you act on principle? One meta-analysis of 185 studies found

00:00:32.528 --> 00:00:36.187
that trust built on care predicted others' ratings of performance

00:00:36.187 --> 00:00:39.846
more strongly than trust built on reliability alone. Competence earns

00:00:39.846 --> 00:00:41.310
trust. Care earns more.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:45.020
Sooner or later, every leader breaks trust. A promise slips

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.380
under a deadline. A decision looks like favoritism. A problem

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.740
someone raised goes nowhere. What happens next depends first on

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.420
what people believe went wrong.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.263
In two experiments, participants judged a job candidate accused of

00:00:57.263 --> 00:01:00.757
a prior error. When the error looked like a competence

00:01:00.757 --> 00:01:04.250
problem, an apology repaired trust better than a denial. People

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.743
accept that a capable person can make one mistake. When

00:01:07.743 --> 00:01:11.237
it looked like an integrity problem, admitting fault was more

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:14.730
damaging, because people read one integrity failure as revealing character.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.515
Denial helped there, but only when the evidence later showed

00:01:18.515 --> 00:01:21.951
the person was innocent. When the evidence showed guilt, apology

00:01:21.951 --> 00:01:25.386
still worked better. So the rule is simple. If you

00:01:25.386 --> 00:01:26.760
did it, own it.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:31.062
Owning it is the start. In a trust game with

00:01:31.062 --> 00:01:35.014
262 participants, researchers tested apologies and promises separately. An apology

00:01:35.014 --> 00:01:38.966
alone, with regret but no commitment, made no significant difference

00:01:38.966 --> 00:01:42.918
to how trust recovered. A promise to change sped recovery.

00:01:42.918 --> 00:01:46.870
And a consistent run of trustworthy actions did the most.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.706
The same study found one kind of damage that never

00:01:50.706 --> 00:01:54.191
fully healed. When bad behavior came with deception, trust never

00:01:54.191 --> 00:01:57.677
fully recovered, even after a promise, an apology and a

00:01:57.677 --> 00:02:01.163
long run of good behavior. Under pressure, a partial account

00:02:01.163 --> 00:02:04.649
or a convenient explanation can feel harmless. It can cost

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:06.740
trust you will not get back.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.302
Put this together, and repair has a shape. Find out

00:02:10.302 --> 00:02:13.514
what people think went wrong. Give a full, accurate account.

00:02:13.514 --> 00:02:16.726
Apologize for the specific failure. Make a specific promise about

00:02:16.726 --> 00:02:19.939
what will change, and when. Address the cause, not only

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:23.151
the symptom. Then keep that promise, especially when keeping it

00:02:23.151 --> 00:02:26.363
costs you time. That is the moment people use to

00:02:26.363 --> 00:02:28.290
decide whether your word means anything.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.779
A note on limits. Most trust-repair studies are lab experiments,

00:02:31.779 --> 00:02:34.917
often with students. They describe how people reason about trust,

00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:38.055
and that reasoning shows up at work. But no study

00:02:38.055 --> 00:02:41.194
can tell you exactly how your team will respond. Watch

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:44.332
what people do after you act. That is the evidence

00:02:44.332 --> 00:02:44.960
that counts.
